questions.question:
In a low-latency steering control microcontroller, an Engineer relies on a hardware-interrupt-driven digital filter to manage sensor noise. Analyze the following embedded C snippet. What is the primary functional consequence of the logic inside the Interrupt Service Routine (ISR)?

```c
volatile uint8_t isr_flag = 0;
int sensor_val = 0;
int filtered_val = 0;

void handle_sensor_irq(void) {
    sensor_val = ADC_get_sample();
    filtered_val = (3*filtered_val + sensor_val) >> 2; // Moving average logic
    isr_flag = 1;
}
```

A) The computed `filtered_val` becomes structurally volatile, resulting in a compiler warning or warning prompt for non-standard `volatile` types.
B) The ISR violates atomicity, leading to inconsistent states if the main loop reads `filtered_val` concurrently while the IRQ executes on another CPU core.
C) The `isr_flag` is never cleared by the main loop logic shown in the snippet, which will cause every subsequent legitimate sensor interrupt to fail to trigger, effectively freezing the steering system.
D) The displacement of the logic to the ISR does not block the main loop, creating a deadlock-free architecture but resulting in an instruction cache miss every time the ADC sample is converted.

